  ],
  "text": "When a bank gives you a loan, it does not move money out of a vault. It types a number into your account. You picture a bank as a box that holds other people's money and lends some of it out. It does hold deposits. But lending is not moving them anywhere. The loan creates a new deposit that did not exist a second earlier. Which means most money in circulation was created by somebody borrowing. You spend it, the shop deposits it, and that bank can lend against it again. Round and round, and the total money supply climbs each time it goes round. Which is also why bank runs happen. If everyone withdraws at once, the cash genuinely is not there. It was never there. It is a record, in a system that assumes not everybody asks at once. Most money is not printed. It is typed, by somebody agreeing to pay it back. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
